A token-bucket rate limiter is a type of rate limiter that uses a fixed number of tokens to control access to a resource. Tokens are added to the bucket at a constant rate, and when a request comes in, the bucket checks if there are enough tokens to allow the request. If there are, the tokens are consumed and the request is allowed to proceed. If there are not, the request is denied and the bucket is blocked until the tokens are available.
Here's an example of how a token-bucket rate limiter works in Go:
```go
package main
import (
	"fmt"
	"time"
)
type TokenBucket struct {
	tokens int
	rate   float64
}
func NewTokenBucket(tokens int, rate float64) *TokenBucket {
	return &TokenBucket{
		tokens: tokens,
		rate:   rate,
	}
}
func (t *TokenBucket) AddTokens(tokens int) {
	t.tokens += tokens
}
func (t *TokenBucket) ConsumeTokens(tokens int) {
	if t.tokens >= tokens {
		t.tokens -= tokens
	} else {
		fmt.Println("Rate limit exceeded")
	}
}
